 "label": "NOT_SUPPORTED",
 "rationale": "The claim asserts that Phil Rudd became the *only* Australian-born member of AC/DC after the bassist of their third LP left. The evidence identifies Mark Evans as the bassist on the third LP (High Voltage, 1976) and confirms he left the band before Cliff Williams joined. However, the evidence explicitly states that vocalist Bon Scott was born in Scotland (Forfar, Angus, Scotland), meaning he was not Australian-born. Since Bon Scott was a member of the band during the period in question (after Evans left and before his death in 1980), Phil Rudd was not the *only* Australian-born member (the Young brothers were also Australian-born, but the claim fails primarily because Scott was non-Australian, making the set of non-Australian members include Scott, or rather, the claim implies everyone else was non-Australian or that Rudd was the sole Australian, which is false because the Young brothers are Australian. More critically, the claim says Rudd became the *only* Australian-born member. The Young brothers are Australian. So Rudd was never the *only* Australian-born member. But even if we interpret 'only' as 'the only one remaining from a specific subset' or similar, the presence of Bon Scott (non-Australian) doesn't contradict Rudd being Australian. The contradiction is that the Young brothers are also Australian-born. The claim says Rudd became the *only* Australian-born member. This is false because Angus and Malcolm Young are Australian-born. Additionally, the evidence shows Bon Scott was Scottish, so he is not Australian. The claim fails because there were other Australian-born members (the Youngs).",
 "sources": [
  "AC/DC discography\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AC/DC_discography",
  "Bon Scott\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bon_Scott"
 ]
}

